package law

conservation_of_electric_charge_law: {
  content: #"""
    The *law of conservation of electric charge* states that the total electric
    charge of an isolated system remains constant. Charges can move from one
    part of the system to another, but the system's total charge does not
    change.

    For example, when two initially neutral objects are charged by rubbing,
    electrons move from one object to the other. The object that loses
    electrons becomes positively charged, and the one that gains them becomes
    negatively charged. Their charges are equal in magnitude and opposite in
    sign, so the total charge of the pair remains zero.

    The law applies to the whole isolated system. If we look at only one part
    and charge flows into or out of it, that part's charge can change; the
    charge of the larger system that includes the surroundings remains
    conserved.
  """#
  related: ["concept/quantization-of-electric-charge"]
}
